---
/**
 * BaseLayout v2 — the shell of every public page.
 *
 * PROPS. `title` and `description` are required. Everything else is optional and defaults to the
 * v1 behaviour, so the five policy pages that were written against v1 (`title` + `description`
 * only, with their own "Reviewed <date>" line) keep working unchanged and adopt `kind="policy"` and
 * `reviewedAt` when their owner is ready.
 *
 * THE HEAD, AND WHY EACH TAG IS CONDITIONAL.
 *
 * · **Icons** come from `scripts/assets/asset-manifest.mjs` — the SVG favicon, the ICO for legacy
 *   agents, and the 180px Apple touch icon. This replaces v1's `<link rel="icon" href="data:,">`
 *   placeholder, which is what lets `seo-engineer` drop `img-src data:` from the CSP.
 * · **The web app manifest** is linked because it now exists: `apps/web/public/manifest.webmanifest`
 *   (`seo-engineer`), guarded by `node scripts/seo/verify-manifest.mjs`. It declares
 *   `display: "browser"` — no service worker and no offline shell ship yet, so nothing here claims
 *   an installed app that works offline. `manifest-src` is not enumerated in the served policy; it
 *   falls back to `default-src 'self'`, which admits this same-origin file.
 * · **Fonts** are preloaded, both faces, `crossorigin` — they are same-origin `woff2` and a preload
 *   without `crossorigin` fetches the file twice.
 * · **Open Graph needs an ABSOLUTE url.** `Astro.site` is undefined until `PUBLIC_SITE_URL` is
 *   configured, and a site-relative `og:image` is not resolvable by a crawler. So with no site
 *   configured, `og:image`, `og:image:alt`, the dimensions and `twitter:image` are OMITTED and
 *   `twitter:card` stays `summary`; with one configured they are emitted absolute and the card
 *   becomes `summary_large_image`. Canonical and `og:url` follow the same rule, for the stronger
 *   reason that a canonical pointing at a guessed host is worse than no canonical at all.
 * · **`og:image:alt`** carries both strings baked into the card artwork — `docs/ACCESSIBILITY.md`
 *   F22. It is rendered here rather than by `seo-engineer` because this layout owns the head.
 * · **`theme-color`** is per colour scheme, matching the two surfaces the stylesheet paints.
 * · **`/theme-init.js`** is a blocking classic script, deliberately: see the endpoint's own
 *   docblock. It is the only script in `<head>` and it is external, so `script-src 'self'` admits
 *   it with no hash and no header change.
 *
 * MOTION AND ADS BY KIND. `policy` and `utility` pages get no ambient motif, no scroll reveal and
 * no ad insertion point — ADR 0003 rule 1 excludes error, maintenance and status pages from the
 * allowance, and `AGENTS.md §4` forbids an ad on a privacy or terms page outright. The `kind` prop
 * is what a page declares that with; it is emitted as `data-page-kind` so the rule is inspectable
 * in the built HTML and testable by `apps/web/scripts/verify-dist.mjs`.
 *
 * STRUCTURED DATA. One `<script type="application/ld+json">` per indexable page, built by
 * `src/lib/seo/schema.ts` (`seo-engineer`) and rendered here because this layout owns the `<head>`
 * (`docs/SEO.md §6.5`). This layout composes nothing: the graph, the allowlist and the refusals are
 * that module's, it validates its own output and throws, and a block that broke the policy would
 * fail the build rather than ship. What is emitted is the sitewide identity — `Organization` and
 * `WebSite` — whose only prose claim is the wordmark "DelayPilot", which every page renders as text
 * in the header and the footer. `Article` and `ItemList` are built and have no call site yet
 * (`docs/SEO.md §12` item 9); this layout does not invent one.
 */
import '../styles/tokens.css'
import '@delaypilot/ui/primitives.css'
import './app.css'

import { home } from '../lib/copy/home.ts'
import { nav } from '../lib/copy/nav.ts'
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro'
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro'
import { pages } from '../lib/copy/pages.ts'
import { pageSchemaJson } from '../lib/seo/schema.ts'
import { COMMITTED_ORIGIN } from '../lib/seo/site-url.mjs'
import { bingSiteVerification, googleSiteVerification } from '../lib/seo/verification.ts'

interface Props {
  title: string
  description: string
  /**
   * Current nav item key so exactly one link carries `aria-current="page"`.
   *
   * `| undefined` is explicit because `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` is on (`AGENTS.md §3.1`): a page
   * that forwards an optional value must be able to pass `undefined` through without a cast.
   */
  nav?: string | undefined
  /** Marketing and article pages may carry ambient motion; policy and utility pages may not. */
  kind?: 'marketing' | 'policy' | 'article' | 'utility' | undefined
  robots?: 'index' | 'noindex' | undefined
  /** ISO date. Rendered as "Reviewed <date>" for policy and article pages. */
  reviewedAt?: string | undefined
}

const {
  title,
  description,
  nav: current,
  kind = 'marketing',
  robots = 'index',
  reviewedAt,
} = Astro.props

/**
 * THE ERROR DOCUMENT HAS NO ADDRESS OF ITS OWN.
 *
 * Astro builds every route to `<path>/index.html`, with one exception: its error documents are
 * emitted as bare `/404.html` and `/500.html`, and that file is served as the BODY OF A 404
 * RESPONSE to whatever address the reader actually asked for. `/404/` is not a route this site
 * serves, so a self-referencing canonical there names an address that does not resolve — a dead
 * reference (`AGENTS.md §1.6`), and a canonical is the one tag a crawler acts on without asking.
 *
 * MEASURED, NOT PREDICTED. A build with `PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://delaypilot.app` on 2026-09-20
 * emitted twenty canonicals; nineteen were correct self-references and the twentieth was
 * `https://delaypilot.app/404/` (`docs/SEO.md §10.1`). `pnpm test:seo` fails that case by name,
 * `canonical.on-error-document`.
 *
 * `/500` is named alongside `/404` because it is the same class of file, not because the route
 * exists: if one is ever added, it inherits the rule instead of re-finding this defect.
 */
const ERROR_DOCUMENTS = new Set(['/404', '/500'])
const isErrorDocument = ERROR_DOCUMENTS.has(Astro.url.pathname.replace(/\/$/, ''))

/**
 * A canonical URL and an `og:url` need an absolute origin, which comes from `PUBLIC_SITE_URL`
 * through `site` in `astro.config.mjs`. That variable is deliberately undefined until a real domain
 * is configured, so both tags are omitted rather than emitted against a guessed origin: inventing
 * one is exactly the fabricated value `AGENTS.md §1.1` forbids.
 *
 * `og:image` and `twitter:image` follow the error document too: a shared card belongs to a page a
 * reader can arrive at and share, and this one is a response body, not a page.
 */
const canonicalUrl =
  Astro.site && !isErrorDocument ? new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site).href : undefined
const ogImage =
  Astro.site && !isErrorDocument ? new URL('/og/default-1200x630.png', Astro.site).href : undefined

/**
 * The JSON-LD graph for this page, or `undefined` where markup does not belong.
 *
 * WHICH PAGES. Indexable ones only. Structured data is a search surface, and a page that is not in
 * the index has none; `pnpm test:seo` fails a block on a `noindex` page by name
 * (`schema.on-noindex-page`). That excludes the error document, which is `noindex` already.
 *
 * WHICH ORIGIN, AND WHY A BUILD WITH NO `PUBLIC_SITE_URL` STILL EMITS. `@id` and `url` have no form
 * a crawler resolves except an absolute one, which is the same reason `sitemap.xml`, the `Sitemap:`
 * line in `robots.txt` and `llms.txt` all write `COMMITTED_ORIGIN` on every build, configured or
 * not (`docs/SEO.md §3`). This block therefore asserts nothing the built artifact does not already
 * assert three times over, and it asserts it about the ORGANISATION and the SITE — not about this
 * page's address, which is what a canonical claims and why that tag is still omitted. When
 * `PUBLIC_SITE_URL` is set, both come from it, so the two can never disagree.
 */
const schemaOrigin = Astro.site ? Astro.site.origin : COMMITTED_ORIGIN
const schemaJson =
  robots === 'noindex'
    ? undefined
    : pageSchemaJson({ origin: schemaOrigin, pathname: Astro.url.pathname })

/**
 * `docs/ACCESSIBILITY.md` F22: both strings baked into the card artwork, as text.
 *
 * COMPOSED FROM THE TWO EXPORTS, NOT RETYPED. It was a literal here that happened to equal
 * `${nav.brand} — ${home.promise}` byte for byte, which is a rendered string living outside
 * `lib/copy/**` and one edit away from contradicting the artwork it describes (copy review F-8).
 * The joiner is the same unspaced em dash `DIRECTIVE.md §7` writes the support line with.
 */
const ogImageAlt = `${nav.brand} — ${home.promise}`

const reviewedDate =
  reviewedAt === undefined
    ? undefined
    : new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', {
        day: 'numeric',
        month: 'long',
        year: 'numeric',
        timeZone: 'UTC',
      }).format(new Date(`${reviewedAt}T00:00:00Z`))
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    {robots === 'noindex' && <meta name="robots" content="noindex" />}
    {canonicalUrl && <link rel="canonical" href={canonicalUrl} />}

    {
      /*
       * SEARCH CONSOLE AND BING VERIFICATION — `docs/SEO.md §10.3`.
       *
       * Rendered only when a token is configured. `src/lib/seo/verification.ts` has already refused
       * anything that is not 8 to 128 characters of `A-Za-z0-9_-.:=`, so nothing here can break out
       * of the attribute it is written into, and an empty or invented token resolves to `undefined`
       * and emits no tag: a verification that silently never completes is worse than none.
       * `pnpm test:seo` fails if a token is set and no page carries the tag.
       */
    }
    {
      googleSiteVerification && (
        <meta name="google-site-verification" content={googleSiteVerification} />
      )
    }
    {bingSiteVerification && <meta name="msvalidate.01" content={bingSiteVerification} />}

    <link
      rel="preload"
      as="font"
      type="font/woff2"
      href="/fonts/geist-sans-latin-400.woff2"
      crossorigin
    />
    <link
      rel="preload"
      as="font"
      type="font/woff2"
      href="/fonts/geist-sans-latin-600.woff2"
      crossorigin
    />

    <link rel="icon" href="/icons/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/icons/favicon.ico" sizes="16x16 32x32 48x48" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/icons/apple-touch-icon-180.png" />
    <link rel="manifest" href="/manifest.webmanifest" />

    <meta name="theme-color" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" content="#eef5fb" />
    <meta name="theme-color" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" content="#07111f" />

    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="DelayPilot" />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    {canonicalUrl && <meta property="og:url" content={canonicalUrl} />}
    {
      ogImage && (
        <Fragment>
          <meta property="og:image" content={ogImage} />
          <meta property="og:image:alt" content={ogImageAlt} />
          <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200" />
          <meta property="og:image:height" content="630" />
        </Fragment>
      )
    }

    <meta name="twitter:card" content={ogImage ? 'summary_large_image' : 'summary'} />
    <meta name="twitter:title" content={title} />
    <meta name="twitter:description" content={description} />
    {ogImage && <meta name="twitter:image" content={ogImage} />}
    {ogImage && <meta name="twitter:image:alt" content={ogImageAlt} />}

    {
      /*
       * `is:inline` so Astro leaves the element exactly as authored — it must not be treated as a
       * module, bundled, or given a build-time attribute. `set:html` so the body is written as the
       * JSON text it is rather than HTML-escaped into `&quot;`, which no JSON parser accepts.
       * `pageSchemaJson` has already replaced every `<` with its six-character JSON unicode
       * escape, so the element cannot be closed early; that is the whole injection surface of a data
       * block, and it is handled at the source rather than trusted to this call site.
       *
       * A JSON-LD block is DATA, not executable script. Measured under the served
       * `script-src 'self'` policy with no hash sources: zero CSP violations and zero console
       * errors, while an executable inline `<script>` in the same page produces one of each
       * (`docs/SEO.md §6.2`). `apps/web/scripts/verify-dist.mjs` carries that exception scoped to
       * this `type` and to nothing else, with a paired negative case in its `--self-test`.
       */
    }
    {schemaJson && <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={schemaJson} />}

    <script is:inline src="/theme-init.js"></script>
  </head>

  <body class="dpx-body" data-page-kind={kind}>
    <a class="dpx-skip-link" href="#main">{nav.skipLink}</a>

    <SiteHeader current={current} />

    {
      /*
       * `tabindex="-1"` so the skip link's target takes focus itself.
       *
       * In Chromium the link already worked fully without it — measured at 375 and 1440: the hash
       * is set, the page scrolls, and the next Tab lands on the first control inside `main`. That
       * is a browser behaviour verifiable in exactly one browser; the attribute removes the
       * dependence on it (`docs/ACCESSIBILITY.md` F33, SC 2.4.1).
       */
    }
    <main class="dpx-main" id="main" tabindex="-1">
      <slot name="page-header" />
      {
        reviewedDate !== undefined && (
          <div class="dpx-shell">
            <p class="dpx-article__meta">
              <span>
                {pages.article.reviewedLabel} <time datetime={reviewedAt}>{reviewedDate}</time>
              </span>
            </p>
          </div>
        )
      }
      <slot />
    </main>

    <SiteFooter current={current} />
  </body>
</html>
